# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Never commit the result — the repository
# `.gitignore` covers `.env` and `.env.*`, and this file is the one exception.
#
# Anything prefixed `EXPO_PUBLIC_` is inlined into the JavaScript bundle at
# build time, which means it ships to the device and can be read by anyone who
# unzips the app. That is fine for the values below — a base URL and OAuth
# client IDs are public identifiers by design. It is never fine for a secret.
# A client secret, an API key, a signing key: none of those belong in this file
# or anywhere else in `app/`. They live in `backend/.env`.

# --- What the code reads today ---

# Where the API is.
#
# Optional in development: with this unset, the app takes the host it already
# reached Metro on and points at port 8000, so it follows your laptop onto
# whatever network it joins. Set it to aim somewhere else — a backend on a
# different port, or staging.
#
# Required in any build that is not a development build. Without it the app
# throws at startup naming this variable, rather than defaulting to something
# plausible that fails later as a connection timeout on a user's phone.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.0.5:8000

# --- Not read yet. Listed because they are what the sign-in screen will need. ---
#
# Nothing in `src/` reads these; setting them today changes nothing. They are
# here so that the values you collect from the Google and Apple consoles have a
# named home, and so that the shape of what is needed is visible before the
# screen exists.
#
# The backend pins provider tokens to a single `aud` per provider
# (`GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` in `backend/.env`), so whichever client ID the app asks
# Google to mint tokens for must be the same one the backend is configured
# with. See `docs/onboarding.md`.

# The OAuth client of type **Web application**, from Google Cloud Console.
# Despite the name it is not for a website: it is what `webClientId` means to
# the native SDK, it is required on both platforms to get an ID token at all,
# and it is the `aud` those tokens carry. So it is the same string that
# `backend/.env` holds as GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID — one audience, both platforms, no
# per-platform branching in the verifier.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=

# The OAuth client of type **iOS**, from the same project. Needed by the native
# SDK on iOS to start the flow at all; its reversed form is also the URL scheme
# in `app.json`. Android needs an OAuth client of its own too — registered
# against the signing certificate's SHA-1 — but nothing references it by value,
# so it has no variable here. Its absence shows up as a bare DEVELOPER_ERROR.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=

# The RevenueCat public SDK key for the Android app, from RevenueCat →
# Project → Apps → the Play app → "Public app-specific API key" (it starts
# `goog_`). An identifier rather than a secret — it ships in the bundle by
# design. With it unset the buy screen keeps `unconfiguredStore()`: plans and
# packs still render, nothing is priced, nothing can be bought.
#
# iOS gets a sibling (`appl_`) when #99 brings the platform up.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_REVENUECAT_ANDROID_KEY=
